 ## Overview
-ObjectNet Training introduces a category-specific prior by inserting a dedicated network (ObjectNet) before the PatchNet to regress patch latent codes and extrinsics from a shared object latent code [S1]. This architecture increases network capacity and establishes coarse geometric correspondences across different instances of the same category [S1]. It is distinct from standard PatchNet training by decoupling the object-level identity from patch-level details via a two-stage latent regression process [S1].
+ObjectNet Training introduces a category-specific prior by inserting a dedicated network (ObjectNet) before PatchNet to regress patch latent codes and extrinsics from a shared object latent code [S1]. This architecture increases network capacity and establishes coarse geometric correspondences across different instances of the same category [S1]. It is distinct from standard PatchNet training by decoupling object-level identity from patch-level details via a three-phase latent regression process [S1].
 
 ## How it works
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Inputs**:
-    *   Object latent code $z_{obj} \in \mathbb{R}^{256}$ [S1].
-    *   Pretrained PatchNet parameters (fixed) [S1].
-    *   Category-specific initialization data (extrinsics of an arbitrary object) [S1].
-*   **Outputs**:
-    *   Patch latent codes for all patches [S1].
-    *   Extrinsics (position and scale) for all patches [S1].
+* **Inputs**: Object latent code $z_{obj} \in \mathbb{R}^{256}$ [S1]. Pretrained PatchNet parameters (fixed) [S1]. Category-specific initialization data containing the extrinsics of an arbitrary object from the target category [S1].
+* **Outputs**: Patch latent codes for all patches [S1]. Regressed extrinsics (position and scale) for all patches [S1].
 
 ### Data Structures
-*   **ObjectNet**: A Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) consisting of 4 fully connected layers [S1].
